{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LcVo4M45KbZB8yUJb9mVX1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The slots are base64-encoded on the Elixir side, and `getRawSlots` in `hooks.ts` decodes them via `fromUtf8Base64`. So the slot values passed to the renderer are already decoded HTML strings. The React renderer's `htmlSlotsToReact` converts HTML strings to React elements using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.\n\nThe issue is that the slot function returns a React element (the span with dangerouslySetInnerHTML), and the component tries to call `defaultSlot()`. But the slot is already a React element, not a function. Let me look at how slots are passed to React components.\n\nLooking at the React renderer's `mount`:\n```typescript\nconst inner = createElement(ctx.component as any, {\n  ...props,\n  ...mapValues(slots, slotFn => slotFn()),\n  live: ctx.hook,\n})\n```\n\nWait — `mapValues(slots, slotFn => slotFn())` — the slots ARE functions (from `htmlSlotsToReact`), and they're being called here to produce React elements. So the props passed to the component include `default: <span>...</span>` (a React element), not a function.\n\nBut in my slot_test.tsx, I'm treating `default` as a function `default?: () => any` and calling `defaultSlot()`. That's wrong — it should be a React element, not a function."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1436,"cache_read_input_tokens":97710,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1436},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5cbf92b-6106-41f2-ab9b-5f76d18aa231","uuid":"7d853f4e-cf7f-446e-bcd9-fdf559d1e82a"}
