{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013hQtA4jVSwrkurnRxJ7SBL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The counter div contains only `<!---->` (Vue empty placeholder), which means Vue mounted but the component rendered nothing. The body has height 0 because there's no visible content.\n\nThis means the Vue counter component was found and mounted by Vue, but it renders empty. This is likely because the Vue component module (from `import.meta.glob`) is the full module object `{ default: Component }`, and Vue receives the module wrapper instead of the actual component. Vue might handle this differently than React — Vue's `createApp` receives a component options object or a function, and a module wrapper `{ default: ... }` is not a valid component.\n\nLet me check how the Vue resolve function works. In the original code, `findComponent` returns the raw glob entry. For Vue files with eager import, Vite returns `{ default: ComponentDefinition, ... }`. Vue's `h()` function might handle `{ default: ... }` differently.\n\nActually wait — looking at the `app.js` setup function:\n```js\nsetup: (createApp, component, props, slots, plugin, el) => {\n    const app = createApp({ render: () => h(component, props, slots) })\n```\n\nThe `component` is what `resolve` returns, which is `findComponent(vueComponents, name)`. For Vue, `import.meta.glob(\"**/*.vue\", { eager: true })` returns objects where the value has a `.default` property that IS the component (SFC compilation result). The old code passed this module object to `h(component, ...)` — and Vue's `h()` function can handle component objects with `.default` (it auto-unwraps).\n\nHmm, but now `findComponent` strips the `.vue` extension and matches — this should still work the same way. Unless my `findComponent` change broke the Vue resolution. Let me check what `findComponent` returns for a Vue component now.\n\nActually, let me re-read my `findComponent` change:"}],"s