{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014uPHddNyz2UXE5H615Kgrp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The debugging approach is getting complicated. Let me take a different approach — the Vue counter div has `<!---->` as content, which means Vue mounted the component but it rendered empty. The Vue component might be receiving the wrong data. Let me look at the actual counter.vue to understand what it expects, and whether the module wrapper issue applies to Vue.\n\nActually, the more likely issue is that Vue's `h()` function is receiving the module object `{ default: ... }` and trying to render it. With Vue 3, `h()` with a plain object containing a `default` key will try to render it as a component with a `default` slot — it won't auto-resolve `default` as a component. This would result in an empty render.\n\nThe same `findComponent` is used for both Vue and React resolvers. Vue's glob returns `{ default: VueComponent }` and React's glob returns `{ default: ReactComponent }`. The old code (before my changes) also returned the same thing, so this can't be the cause.\n\nWait — let me check if the Vue test was also broken BEFORE my changes. The key question is: did the Vue E2E tests ever pass on this NixOS machine?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1895,"cache_read_input_tokens":125024,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1895},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5cbf92b-6106-41f2-ab9b-5f76d18aa231","uuid":"c1ca1719-2393-4e24-8862-ebd09e307ae5"}
